 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Depends on the number of samples $N$ and the cost of triplane feature gathering and MLP inference. Inverse CDF sampling adds overhead compared to uniform sampling [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity**: Dominated by the triplane storage ($512 \times 512 \times 32 \times 3$ floats) and MLP weights [S1].
-- **Strengths**: Tight surface extraction, lower rendering variance with stratification, high-fidelity geometry [S1].
-- **Limitations**: Requires careful initialization and regularization of $\beta$. Inverse CDF sampling is computationally more expensive than uniform sampling [S1].
-- **When to Use**: When high-fidelity geometry and accurate surface extraction are required, such as in 3D GANs or detailed scene reconstruction [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of samples $N$ and the cost of the MLP evaluations. The use of inverse CDF sampling concentrates samples near the surface, potentially reducing the number of samples needed for high-fidelity geometry compared to uniform sampling [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the triplane features (e.g., $3 \times 512 \times 512 \times 32$ floats) and the MLP weights [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Tight surface extraction due to the SDF representation; efficient sampling near the surface via inverse CDF; lower rendering variance with stratification [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful initialization (e.g., SAL initialization) to avoid degenerate SDFs early in training [S1]. The spatially varying $\beta$ requires regularization to prevent instability [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When high-fidelity geometry extraction is prioritized over texture, and when training a 3D GAN or differentiable renderer [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and simple uniform sampling suffices, or when the scene does not have well-defined surfaces [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method builds on VolSDF [72] and StyleSDF [45], improving upon NeRF [38] and EG3D [7] by using a Laplacian CDF for opacity transformation and stratified sampling [S1]. It was introduced in "What You See is What You GAN" (CVPR 2024) [S1].
+This method is described in "What You See is What You GAN: Rendering Every Pixel for High-Fidelity Geometry in 3D GANs" [S1]. It builds upon VolSDF [72] and StyleSDF [45], improving upon NeRF [38] and EG3D [7] by incorporating stratified sampling and a triplane-based SDF representation [S1]. The use of the Laplacian CDF for opacity transformation was previously explored in VolSDF [72], but this method extends it by allowing $\beta$ to vary spatially and integrating it into a GAN framework with triplane conditioning [S1].
 
